<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Pounds and kilograms at a glance</h2>
    <p>1 pound is exactly 0.45359237 kilograms by international agreement, and 1 kilogram is about 2.20462 pounds. These are the conversions people look up most:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Pounds</th><th class="num">Kilograms</th><th>Kilograms</th><th class="num">Pounds</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 lb</td><td class="num">0.454 kg</td><td>1 kg</td><td class="num">2.20 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5 lb</td><td class="num">2.268 kg</td><td>2 kg</td><td class="num">4.41 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 lb</td><td class="num">4.536 kg</td><td>5 kg</td><td class="num">11.02 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>25 lb</td><td class="num">11.34 kg</td><td>10 kg</td><td class="num">22.05 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>50 lb</td><td class="num">22.68 kg</td><td>20 kg</td><td class="num">44.09 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>100 lb</td><td class="num">45.36 kg</td><td>23 kg</td><td class="num">50.71 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>150 lb</td><td class="num">68.04 kg</td><td>50 kg</td><td class="num">110.23 lb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>200 lb</td><td class="num">90.72 kg</td><td>100 kg</td><td class="num">220.46 lb</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>23 kg is in the table for a reason: many airlines set their checked-bag limit at 23 kg, which is the familiar 50 lb rule. The exact figure is 50.71 lb, so a bag that reads 50.5 lb on your home scale is fine, but check the limit on your own ticket.</p>

    <h2>Converting in your head</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kilograms to pounds:</strong> double it, then add 10%. 70 kg → 140 + 14 = 154 lb (exact: 154.3).</li>
      <li><strong>Pounds to kilograms:</strong> halve it, then subtract 10% of the result. 150 lb → 75 − 7.5 = 67.5 kg (exact: 68.04).</li>
      <li><strong>Ounces to grams:</strong> multiply by 28. 4 oz → 112 g (exact: 113.4).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These shortcuts land within about 1 to 2% of the true value, close enough for luggage, groceries, and gym plates. Use the converter for anything you are billed or dosed by.</p>

    <h2>3 units that trip people up</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Fluid ounces are not ounces.</strong> A fluid ounce measures volume, so it only matches an ounce of weight for water and similar liquids. A cup of flour weighs about 4.25 oz, not 8. Convert recipe volumes with the ingredient's own weight per cup.</li>
      <li><strong>Troy ounces are heavier.</strong> Gold and silver are priced per troy ounce, 31.1035 grams, about 9.7% more than the everyday ounce of 28.3495 grams. A "1 oz" bullion coin weighs 1.097 ordinary ounces.</li>
      <li><strong>"Ton" means 3 different things.</strong> A U.S. short ton is 2,000 lb, a British long ton is 2,240 lb, and a metric tonne is 1,000 kg, or 2,204.6 lb. Freight quotes and import paperwork usually mean the metric tonne; U.S. truck scales and landscaping yards usually mean the short ton.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Weight for shipping</h2>
    <p>Carriers such as UPS and FedEx round any fraction of a pound up to the next whole pound, so a 2.1 lb package is billed as 3 lb. Large, light boxes can also be billed by size instead of weight. The <a href="dimensional-weight.html">dimensional weight calculator</a> shows which number a carrier will charge you for.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Weight conversion questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How many pounds are in a kilogram?</summary><p>1 kilogram is 2.20462 pounds. For quick math, double the kilograms and add 10%.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How many grams are in an ounce?</summary><p>An everyday (avoirdupois) ounce is 28.3495 grams. A troy ounce, used for precious metals, is 31.1035 grams.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is 23 kg in pounds?</summary><p>23 kilograms is 50.71 pounds. It is a common checked-bag limit, often quoted as 50 lb.</p></details>
  </section>
